<h4>Local files and syncing</h4>
<ul>
    <li>Obsidian works with <strong>local files</strong>, which is an advantage for work where security matters. File paths are exposed as-is, so computers handle them easily and quickly.</li>
    <li><strong>Avoid putting in files other than markdown (HTML, PDF, images, Hangul documents, and so on)</strong> — Obsidian's rendering slows down.</li>
    <li>For syncing you can use the official Sync, or build a shared vault within an organization using a plugin such as <code>osync</code>, which mimics it and can be <strong>self-hosted</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>What Goes In — Knowledge Capture</h3>
<p>"If I just use a recent model with web search, why bother organizing things locally?" is a natural question. The answer lies in <strong>the limits of our own heads</strong> and in <strong>learning</strong>.</p>

<h4>A forward base for learning — the stretched goal</h4>
<p>The <strong>stretched goal</strong> from educational technology is the idea that learning happens smoothly only when you set a goal you can reach by stretching your arm a little further. The LLM wiki plays the role of this <strong>forward base for learning</strong>. You capture material you do not understand yet but want to learn, and inch forward with the help of AI.</p>

<h4>Let AI know what you care about</h4>
<p>Like the links and articles you pile up in KakaoTalk's "send to myself," we capture <strong>knowledge we have taken an interest in</strong>. Artificial intelligence does not know all of your tastes, but once you let it know <strong>where you showed interest and what you applied your human bias to</strong>, there is far more it can learn from there.</p>

<h4>What goes into the main vault vs. the wiki vault</h4>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Main vault</strong> — concepts you <strong>know well</strong> enough to lecture on all day.</li>
    <li><strong>LLM wiki</strong> — concepts you want to talk about all day but <strong>do not know well yet</strong>. Often the more technical material.</li>
</ul>
<p>For example, overseas cases researched while looking into mounting Starlink on a vehicle, or concepts learned while writing up BM25 (Best Matching 25, lexicon-based search), go into the wiki.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Key point</span>
    What matters is organizing the concepts you intend to study <strong>in a form that not only you can read, but artificial intelligence can read alongside you</strong>.
</div>
